sec.p:
We consider the following QIF model:
(1a)
dvdt=a(v−vSN)2+c(Iapp−ISN,0−Ipump), \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \frac{\mathrm{d} v}{\mathrm{~d} t}=a\left(v-v_{\mathrm{SN}}\right)^{2}+c\left(I_{\mathrm{app}}-I_{\mathrm{SN}, 0}-I_{\mathrm{pump}}\right), \tag{1a} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(1b)
ifv>vth, thenv←vr. \begin{document}$$\displaystyle v \leftarrow v_{\mathrm{r}}$$\end{document}
Its branch of fixed points, defined by c(Iapp − ISN,0 − Ipump) = −a(v − vSN)2, folds at (Iapp = ISN,0 + Ipump, v = vSN). Stable limit cycles emerge via a SNIC bifurcation when the reset voltage vr is smaller than vSN (Fig. 2c), while they emerge via a homoclinic bifurcation at Iapp=ISN,0+Ipump−ac(vr−vSN)2\begin{document}$I_{\mathrm{app}}=I_{\mathrm{SN}, 0}+I_{\mathrm{pump}}-\frac{a}{c}\left(v_{\mathrm{r}}-v_{\mathrm{SN}}\right)^{2}$\end{document} when vr > vSN (Fig. 2d), with a SNL bifurcation at vr = vSN. Let us first determine, for a given [K+]o, parameter values of System (1) such that its bifurcation diagram with respect to the applied current closely resembles that of the fast subsystem of the target model.

ISN,0 is the value of applied current Iapp at which the saddle-node bifurcation of interest occurs in the fast subsystem of the target model, in the absence of pump current. vSN is the membrane potential at the saddle-node. We take a and c to be the quadratic normal form coefficients for the saddle-node bifurcation. They are defined as (see, for example, [19]):
a=12pTB(q,q),c=pT∂F∂Iapp, \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} & a=\frac{1}{2} p^{\mathrm{T}} B(q, q), \\ & c=p^{\mathrm{T}} \frac{\partial F}{\partial I_{\mathrm{app}}},\end{aligned}$$\end{document}
where p and q are the normalized left and right eigenvectors of the Jacobian matrix and B the Hessian quadratic form of the vector field F=(dvdt,dhdt,dndt)T\begin{document}$F=\left(\frac{\mathrm{d} v}{\mathrm{~d} t}, \frac{\mathrm{~d} h}{\mathrm{~d} t}, \frac{\mathrm{~d} n}{\mathrm{~d} t}\right)^{\mathrm{T}}$\end{document} at the saddle-node:
Bi=∑j=1n∑k=1n∂2Fi∂xj∂xkqjqk \begin{document}$$\displaystyle B_{i}=\sum_{j=1}^{n} \sum_{k=1}^{n} \frac{\partial^{2} F_{i}}{\partial x_{j} \partial x_{k}} q_{j} q_{k}$$\end{document}
Since the bias term c is close to 1 cm2 µF−1, we set it to this value for simplicity.

We incorporate these functions of [K+]o in System (1), in which [K+]o now appears as a parameter. Its bifurcation structure with respect to [K+]o and the applied current Iapp closely resembles that of the fast subsystem of the original model (see already Fig. 3). A summary of the reduction procedure up to this stage is provided in the form of an algorithm in Table I. An illustration of this procedure using temperature instead of potassium concentration is shown in Fig. S2. Returning to the potassium example, we then include potassium dynamics to obtain the following model:

(3a)

dvdt=a(v−vSN)2+Iapp−ISN,0([K+]o)−Ipump([K+]o),
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \frac{\mathrm{d} v}{\mathrm{~d} t}=a\left(v-v_{\mathrm{SN}}\right)^{2}+I_{\mathrm{app}}-I_{\mathrm{SN}, 0}\left(\left[\mathrm{~K}^{+}\right]_{\mathrm{o}}\right)-I_{\mathrm{pump}}\left(\left[\mathrm{~K}^{+}\right]_{\mathrm{o}}\right), \tag{3a} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}




(3b)

d[K+]odt=−2γIpump([K+]o),
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \frac{\mathrm{d}\left[\mathrm{~K}^{+}\right]_{\mathrm{o}}}{\mathrm{~d} t}=-2 \gamma I_{\text {pump }}\left(\left[\mathrm{K}^{+}\right]_{\mathrm{o}}\right), \tag{3b} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}




    (3c)

    ifv>vth([K+]o), thenv←vr([K+]o),
    \begin{document}$$\displaystyle v \leftarrow v_{\mathrm{r}}\left(\left[\mathrm{K}^{+}\right]_{\mathrm{o}}\right)$$\end{document}




(3d)

K+]o←[K+]o+ΔK,
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \left.\mathrm{K}^{+}\right]_{\mathrm{o}} \leftarrow\left[\mathrm{~K}^{+}\right]_{\mathrm{o}}+\Delta_{\mathrm{K}}, \tag{(3d} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}



with


ISN,0([K+]o)=β0,ISN+β1,ISN[K+]o,vr([K+]o)=β0,vr+β1,vr[K+]ovth([K+]o)=α0,vth1+exp⁡(α1,vth([K+]o−α2,vth))−α3,vth
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} & I_{\mathrm{SN}, 0}\left(\left[\mathrm{~K}^{+}\right]_{\mathrm{o}}\right)=\beta_{0, I_{\mathrm{SN}}}+\beta_{1, I_{\mathrm{SN}}}\left[\mathrm{K}^{+}\right]_{\mathrm{o}}, \\ & v_{\mathrm{r}}\left(\left[\mathrm{K}^{+}\right]_{\mathrm{o}}\right)=\beta_{0, v_{\mathrm{r}}}+\beta_{1, v_{\mathrm{r}}}\left[\mathrm{K}^{+}\right]_{\mathrm{o}} \\ & v_{\mathrm{th}}\left(\left[\mathrm{K}^{+}\right]_{\mathrm{o}}\right)=\frac{\alpha_{0, v_{\mathrm{th}}}}{1+\exp \left(\alpha_{1, v_{\mathrm{th}}}\left(\left[\mathrm{K}^{+}\right]_{\mathrm{o}}-\alpha_{2, v_{\mathrm{th}}}\right)\right)}-\alpha_{3, v_{\mathrm{th}}}\end{aligned}$$\end{document}

The standard slow subsystem of System (3), used to study the dynamics of [K+]o along stable branches of fixed points of the fast subsystem, is given by
0=a(v−vSN)2+Iapp−ISN,0([K+]o)−Ipump([K+]o)d[K+]odt=−2γIpump([K+]o). \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} 0 & =a\left(v-v_{\mathrm{SN}}\right)^{2}+I_{\text {app }}-I_{\mathrm{SN}, 0}\left(\left[\mathrm{~K}^{+}\right]_{\mathrm{o}}\right)-I_{\text {pump }}\left(\left[\mathrm{K}^{+}\right]_{\mathrm{o}}\right) \\ \frac{\mathrm{d}\left[\mathrm{K}^{+}\right]_{\mathrm{o}}}{\mathrm{d} t} & =-2 \gamma I_{\text {pump }}\left(\left[\mathrm{K}^{+}\right]_{\mathrm{o}}\right) .\end{aligned}$$\end{document}
As in the original model, along stable branches of limit cycles of the fast subsystem, the dynamics of [K+]o includes a fast component, causing rapid increments at each spike (here, instantaneous jumps via the reset rule Eq. (3d)). The jump size ΔK is chosen to match the potassium increment per spike in the original model and is therefore small (see already Fig. 6h). Thus, the averaging method can still be employed (see, for example, [8]). The averaged slow subsystem of System (3) is then given by
⟨d[K+]odt⟩=ΔKT([K+]o)−2γIpump([K+]o) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \left\langle\frac{\mathrm{d}\left[\mathrm{K}^{+}\right]_{\mathrm{o}}}{\mathrm{d} t}\right\rangle=\frac{\Delta_{\mathrm{K}}}{T\left(\left[\mathrm{~K}^{+}\right]_{\mathrm{o}}\right)}-2 \gamma I_{\text {pump }}\left(\left[\mathrm{K}^{+}\right]_{\mathrm{o}}\right)$$\end{document}
where T is the period of the limit cycles.

The model obeys the system of differential equations

(4a)

Cdvdt=−INa−IK−IL−Ipump+Iapp,
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} C \frac{\mathrm{~d} v}{\mathrm{~d} t}=-I_{\mathrm{Na}}-I_{\mathrm{K}}-I_{\mathrm{L}}-I_{\mathrm{pump}}+I_{\mathrm{app}}, \tag{4a} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}




(4b)

dhdt=5(αh(1−h)−βhh),
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \frac{\mathrm{d} h}{\mathrm{~d} t}=5\left(\alpha_{h}(1-h)-\beta_{h} h\right), \tag{4b} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}




(4c)

dndt=5(αn(1−n)−βnn),
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \frac{\mathrm{d} n}{\mathrm{~d} t}=5\left(\alpha_{n}(1-n)-\beta_{n} n\right), \tag{4c} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}




(4d)

d[K+]odt=γ(IK−2Ipump),
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \frac{\mathrm{d}\left[\mathrm{~K}^{+}\right]_{\mathrm{o}}}{\mathrm{~d} t}=\gamma\left(I_{\mathrm{K}}-2 I_{\text {pump }}\right), \tag{4d} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}



with currents

(5a)

INa=gNam∞3h(v−ENa),
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} I_{\mathrm{Na}}=g_{\mathrm{Na}} m_{\infty}^{3} h\left(v-E_{\mathrm{Na}}\right), \tag{5a} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}




(5b)

IK=gKn4(v−EK),
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} I_{\mathrm{K}}=g_{\mathrm{K}} n^{4}\left(v-E_{\mathrm{K}}\right), \tag{5b} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}




(5c)

IL=gL(v−EL),
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} I_{\mathrm{L}}=g_{\mathrm{L}}\left(v-E_{\mathrm{L}}\right), \tag{5c} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}




(5d)

Ipump=Ipump,max1+exp⁡(−k([K+]o−Keq)),
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} I_{\mathrm{pump}}=\frac{I_{\mathrm{pump}, \max }}{1+\exp \left(-k\left(\left[\mathrm{~K}^{+}\right]_{\mathrm{o}}-\mathrm{K}_{\mathrm{eq}}\right)\right)}, \tag{5d} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}



and gating functions


m∞=αmαm+βm,αm=0.1−v−35exp⁡(−35−v10)−1,βm=4exp⁡(−v−6018),αh=0.07exp⁡(−v−5820),βh=11+exp⁡(−v−2810),αn=0.01−v−34exp⁡(−v−3410)−1,βn=0.125exp⁡(−v−4480).
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} m_{\infty} & =\frac{\alpha_{m}}{\alpha_{m}+\beta_{m}}, \\ \alpha_{m} & =0.1 \frac{-v-35}{\exp \left(\frac{-35-v}{10}\right)-1}, \\ \beta_{m} & =4 \exp \left(\frac{-v-60}{18}\right), \\ \alpha_{h} & =0.07 \exp \left(\frac{-v-58}{20}\right), \\ \beta_{h} & =\frac{1}{1+\exp \left(\frac{-v-28}{10}\right)}, \\ \alpha_{n} & =0.01 \frac{-v-34}{\exp \left(\frac{-v-34}{10}\right)-1}, \\ \beta_{n} & =0.125 \exp \left(\frac{-v-44}{80}\right) .\end{aligned}$$\end{document}



The reversal potential for the potassium evolves with [K+]o according to


EK=RTFlog⁡([K+]o[K+]i)
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle E_{\mathrm{K}}=\frac{R T}{F} \log \left(\frac{\left[\mathrm{~K}^{+}\right]_{\mathrm{o}}}{\left[\mathrm{K}^{+}\right]_{\mathrm{i}}}\right)$$\end{document}



where R = 8314.4 mJ K−1 mol−1 is the ideal gas constant, F = 96 484.6 C mol−1 the Faraday’s constant and T = 310 K the temperature. The variables and parameter values are listed in Tables III and IV.

The standard slow subsystem of System (4) is given by
(6a)
0=−INa−IK−IL−Ipump+Iapp, \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} 0=-I_{\mathrm{Na}}-I_{\mathrm{K}}-I_{\mathrm{L}}-I_{\mathrm{pump}}+I_{\mathrm{app}}, \tag{6a} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(6b)
0=αh(1−h)−βhh, \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} 0=\alpha_{h}(1-h)-\beta_{h} h, \tag{(6b} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(6c)
0=αn(1−n)−βnn, \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} 0=\alpha_{n}(1-n)-\beta_{n} n, \tag{6c} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(6d)
d[K+]odt=γ(IK−2Ipump). \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \frac{\mathrm{d}\left[\mathrm{~K}^{+}\right]_{\mathrm{o}}}{\mathrm{~d} t}=\gamma\left(I_{\mathrm{K}}-2 I_{\text {pump }}\right) . \tag{6~d} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
As in [13], for [K+]o values such that there is a stable limit cycle in the fast subsystem, we define the averaged slow subsystem of System (4) as
(7a)
⟨d[K+]odt⟩=γ(1T([K+]o)∫0T([K+]o)IK(v([K+]o;τ),n([K+]o;τ),[K+]o)dτ−2Ipump([K+]o)), \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \left\langle\frac{\mathrm{d}\left[\mathrm{~K}^{+}\right]_{\mathrm{o}}}{\mathrm{~d} t}\right\rangle=\gamma\left(\frac{1}{T\left(\left[\mathrm{~K}^{+}\right]_{\mathrm{o}}\right)} \int_{0}^{T\left(\left[\mathrm{~K}^{+}\right]_{\mathrm{o}}\right)} I_{\mathrm{K}}\left(v\left(\left[\mathrm{~K}^{+}\right]_{\mathrm{o}} ; \tau\right), n\left(\left[\mathrm{~K}^{+}\right]_{\mathrm{o}} ; \tau\right),\left[\mathrm{K}^{+}\right]_{\mathrm{o}}\right) \mathrm{d} \tau \quad-2 I_{\mathrm{pump}}\left(\left[\mathrm{~K}^{+}\right]_{\mathrm{o}}\right)\right), \tag{7a} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
where T is the period of the limit cycle.

Let Iapp―\begin{document}$I_{\mathrm{app}}$\end{document}= c(Iapp − ISN,0 − Ipump). If Iapp―>0\begin{document}$I_{\text {app }}>0$\end{document} (tonic spiking region), the period of the limit cycles is given by:


T=1aIapp―(arctan⁡(vth−vSNIappa)−arctan⁡(vr−vSNIappa))
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle T=\frac{1}{\sqrt{a \overline{I_{\mathrm{app}}}}}\left(\arctan \left(\frac{v_{\mathrm{th}}-v_{\mathrm{SN}}}{\sqrt{\frac{I_{\mathrm{app}}}{a}}}\right)-\arctan \left(\frac{v_{\mathrm{r}}-v_{\mathrm{SN}}}{\sqrt{\frac{I_{\mathrm{app}}}{a}}}\right)\right)$$\end{document}



If vr > vSN and −a(vr−vSN)2<Iapp―<0\begin{document}$-a\left(v_{\mathrm{r}}-v_{\mathrm{SN}}\right)^{2}<\overline{I_{\mathrm{app}}}<0$\end{document} (bistable region):


T=log⁡(−2AB(C+1)+AB)2−aIapp―
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle T=\frac{\log \left(-\frac{2 A}{B(C+1)}+\frac{A}{B}\right)}{2 \sqrt{-a \overline{I_{\mathrm{app}}}}}$$\end{document}



with


A=(vr−vSN)−Iapp―a−Iapp―a,B=(vr−vSN)−Iapp―a+Iapp―a,C=vth−vSN−Iapp―a.
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} & A=\left(v_{\mathrm{r}}-v_{\mathrm{SN}}\right) \sqrt{-\frac{\overline{I_{\mathrm{app}}}}{a}}-\frac{\overline{I_{\mathrm{app}}}}{a}, \\ & B=\left(v_{\mathrm{r}}-v_{\mathrm{SN}}\right) \sqrt{-\frac{\overline{I_{\mathrm{app}}}}{a}}+\frac{\overline{I_{\mathrm{app}}}}{a}, \\ & C=\frac{v_{\mathrm{th}}-v_{\mathrm{SN}}}{\sqrt{-\frac{\overline{I_{\mathrm{app}}}}{a}}} .\end{aligned}$$\end{document}



Infinitesimal phase-response curve (iPRC)

Let vr―=vr−vSN.\begin{document}$v_{\mathrm{r}}=v_{\mathrm{r}}-v_{\mathrm{SN}}$\end{document} If Iapp―>0\begin{document}$I_{\text {app }}>0$\end{document}:


iPRC(θ)=cos2⁡(Iapp―aTθ+arctan⁡(vr―Iappa))TIapp―
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \operatorname{iPRC}(\theta)=\frac{\cos ^{2}\left(\sqrt{\overline{I_{\mathrm{app}}} a} T \theta+\arctan \left(\frac{\overline{v_{\mathrm{r}}}}{\sqrt{\frac{I_{\mathrm{app}}}{a}}}\right)\right)}{\overline{T I_{\mathrm{app}}}}$$\end{document}



If vr > vSN and −a(vr−vSN)2<Iapp―<0\begin{document}$-a\left(v_{\mathrm{r}}-v_{\mathrm{SN}}\right)^{2}<\overline{I_{\mathrm{app}}}<0$\end{document} :


iPRC(θ)=−(−Iapp―acosh⁡(−Iapp―aTθ)−vr―sinh⁡(−Iapp―aTθ))2TIapp―(Iapp―a+vr―2)
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \operatorname{iPRC}(\theta)=-\frac{\left(\sqrt{-\frac{\overline{I_{\mathrm{app}}}}{a}} \cosh \left(\sqrt{-\overline{I_{\mathrm{app}}} a} T \theta\right)-\overline{v_{\mathrm{r}}} \sinh \left(\sqrt{-\overline{I_{\mathrm{app}}} a} T \theta\right)\right)^{2}}{T \overline{I_{\mathrm{app}}}\left(\frac{\overline{I_{\mathrm{app}}}}{a}+{\overline{v_{\mathrm{r}}}}^{2}\right)}$$\end{document}

To introduce noise in the applied current, we replace it with:
(9)
Inoisy=Iapp+η, \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} I_{\text {noisy }}=I_{\text {app }}+\eta, \tag{9} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
where ηt is an Ornstein-Uhlenbeck (OU) process defined by
dηt=−1τηηtdt+2σ2τηdWt \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \mathrm{d} \eta_{t}=-\frac{1}{\tau_{\eta}} \eta_{t} \mathrm{~d} t+\sqrt{\frac{2 \sigma^{2}}{\tau_{\eta}}} \mathrm{d} W_{t}$$\end{document}
Wt being the Wiener process. The OU process can be interpreted as a smoothly low-pass filtered white noise. In our simulations, we used a correlation time τη = 20 ms and a standard deviation σ = 0.05 µA cm−2.
